#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Merge a pull request, then PROVE the content reached main. Never infer a
landing from `gh pr merge`'s output.

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
`ci-hub/bin/git-push-verified` closed the same hole for direct pushes, and named
this gap rather than claiming the class closed: it covers `git push`, but HERMIT
LANDINGS GO THROUGH `gh pr merge`, which it cannot reach. That is where the
traffic is -- every drain landing takes this path -- so the false-record risk
sits almost entirely on the route nothing checks.

The measured harm on the neighbouring path: three agents reported a successful
push for a REJECTED push inside about two hours, and a fourth disclosed being one
lapse away. All four had the trap documented. Documentation did not prevent it.
Reading the remote afterwards did.

WHY A MERGE CANNOT BE VERIFIED THE WAY A PUSH IS
------------------------------------------------
This is the part that makes the check non-obvious, and getting it wrong produces
a verifier that fails on every correct landing.

Merge commits are DISABLED on these repositories, so landing squashes or rebases
and **rewrites the sha**. The pull request's head commit never appears on main.
So the push verifier's question -- does the remote ref equal the sha I meant to
land? -- has no answer here, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor <pr-head> main`
is FALSE for a perfectly good landing.

The sound question is about CONTENT: after the merge, is every file the pull
request changed byte-identical on `origin/main`? That is ancestry-independent, it
survives the sha rewrite, and it is what actually matters -- the change is
present or it is not.

⚠️ AND IT IS SCOPED TO THE FILES THE BRANCH TOUCHES, not the files the pull
request is "about". A branch commonly carries more than its headline change --
a formatting fix, a follow-up commit. Checking only the obvious paths reports a
landing that dropped the rest. This walks `git diff --name-only <merge-base>..<head>`.

⚠️ THE STALE PULL-REF HAZARD, WHICH THIS TOOL IS EXPOSED TO BY CONSTRUCTION
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`refs/pull/N/head` can still read the OLD sha for a short window immediately
after a push. Measured twice independently on 2026-08-25, and it broke two pull
requests before it was understood.

This tool resolves the head from exactly that ref, so a read inside the window
compares main against the WRONG head and can report a landing that really
happened as ABSENT. That is the likeliest cause of an exit 3 that surprises you.

So: EXIT 3 MEANS CHECK BY HAND. Re-run after a few seconds; if the verdict
changes, the first answer was the race. Where it matters, confirm from the
branch ref or the API rather than the pull ref -- those converge immediately.
It is deliberately NOT auto-retried here: silently polling would turn a
diagnosable race into a delay nobody can see, and this tool exists to make the
uncertain state visible rather than to paper over it.

THREE VERDICTS PER FILE, BECAUSE TWO DECAY
------------------------------------------
Comparing only "main == pr head" rots: the moment main edits a file for an
unrelated reason, a landing that DID happen starts reporting as missing. Measured
on PR 2288 -- it landed cleanly, another change edited one of its files hours
later, and a two-state check called the whole thing unlanded. Reporting a real
landing as absent is worse than saying nothing, because it invites someone to
land it a second time.

The merge-base blob is what discriminates:

    main == pr head   ARRIVED   the change is present
    main == base      ABSENT    main still holds the pre-change content, so this
                                file's change definitely did not land
    neither           MOVED ON  changed after, or instead. NOT decidable from
                                blobs; a human must read it

So exit 3 distinguishes NOT LANDED (something is ABSENT) from UNDECIDABLE
(nothing absent, but something moved on). Only the first is a claim that the
merge did not arrive.

It also does not verify that the merge was *correct*, only that it *arrived*.

⚠️ VERIFY PROMPTLY. Exit 0 is reachable right after a merge and becomes less
reachable as main advances, because unrelated edits push files into MOVED ON.
That is the tool working, not failing -- but it means this belongs immediately
after the merge, not as a later audit.

WHY `gh-pr-merge-verified` WAS DELETED RATHER THAN KEPT ALONGSIDE
----------------------------------------------------------------
Two verifiers landed for this one gap within a short window, neither with a
first user. Both are recorded here so the deleted one is not reintroduced by
someone who only sees the shape of the hole.

It merged with no pipe, asked GitHub for the merge commit, then fetched and
proved that commit reachable on the base branch. That reachability check is
STRICTLY STRONGER than what this file does: it is ancestry-exact, whereas
content-identity is sound in one direction only -- identical files prove the
change arrived, differing files do not prove it did not.

It was deleted anyway, on one property: IT LEARNS THE MERGE COMMIT FROM THE API.
`gh pr view --json` sits inside its verification path, so on a box where
api.github.com is unreachable it cannot verify at all -- and that box is not
hypothetical. On 2026-08-25 `git ls-remote` succeeded against github.com while
`curl https://api.github.com` failed outright on the same host, and the
condition forced two handoffs. A verifier that cannot run where the landings
happen verifies nothing.

Verification here is PURE GIT: the only `gh` invocation in this file is the
merge itself, and `--verify-only` never reaches it. `gh-merge-verified-test`
asserts that with a tripwire `gh` so the property cannot decay silently.

The stronger ancestry check is worth having back IF it is added as an optional
extra on top of the git-only path, never as the only way to verify.

USAGE
-----
    ci-hub/bin/gh-merge-verified <pr-number> [--repo owner/name] [--verify-only]
                                 [--run-current-main-copy]
                                 [--expect-head <sha>]
                                 [-- extra `gh pr merge` args...]

    # merge, then prove it landed
    ci-hub/bin/gh-merge-verified 2401 --repo rrnewton/hermit

    # only prove: for a merge someone else performed, or from a box whose
    # `gh` cannot reach the API at all (verification is pure git and needs no API)
    ci-hub/bin/gh-merge-verified 2401 --repo rrnewton/hermit --verify-only

    # when this pull request changes gh-merge-verified itself, fetch and run the
    # exact copy from dev-hermit main; that reviewed copy performs admission and
    # the merge, while the modified working-tree copy does neither
    ci-hub/bin/gh-merge-verified 2401 --repo rrnewton/dev-hermit --run-current-main-copy --expect-head <sha>

EXIT
----
    0  merged AND every file the branch touches ARRIVED on origin/main
    1  usage, or a local/remote ref could not be resolved
    2  the `gh pr merge` command itself failed (the case output hid)
    3  the content is NOT all on main. Prints NOT LANDED when a file is ABSENT,
       UNDECIDABLE when files only MOVED ON. Files named either way.
       ⚠️ EXIT 3 MEANS CHECK BY HAND, NOT THAT THE MERGE FAILED. See the stale
       pull-ref hazard below.
    4  COULD NOT DETERMINE: the check did not complete. Distinct from 3 on
       purpose, so a broken verifier is never read as a clean landing.
    5  REFUSED TO MERGE: a CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT names this exact head. Nothing was
       attempted. An APPROVED-AT at the same head does not override it.
    6  REFUSED TO MERGE: a `passed-review-<lane>` label asserts an approval that is
       NOT bound to this head -- the label outlived the sha it was earned at.
       Nothing was attempted. Scoped narrowly: a pull request carrying no
       passed-review label is untouched, so this imposes no new review
       requirement; it only stops a stale cache from admitting.
    7  REFUSED TO MERGE: this tool cannot prove it is the version on dev-hermit main,
       so its own admission checks may be ABSENT from the copy being run. A stale
       lander does not fail -- it SKIPS the checks and reports a clean landing, which
       is indistinguishable from a checked one. `--verify-only` is exempt: it performs
       no merge and so needs no admission. When the reviewed pull request changes
       this file, pass `--run-current-main-copy`; the modified copy fetches and starts
       the exact main copy, and only that main copy performs admission and the merge.
    8  ADMISSION COULD NOT RUN: a pre-merge check could not resolve what it needed
       (comments, reviews or labels), so it can neither admit nor refuse. NOTHING WAS
       ATTEMPTED.
       ⚠️ SPLIT FROM 4 BECAUSE THEY SHARED A BOUNDARY AND NOT A MEANING. An exit code
       passed on from an INNER call carries the inner meaning, not the outer one: the
       admission guard could not resolve, so it said "could not determine", and the
       caller read that as "the MERGE could not be determined". Same code, two
       meanings, one boundary -- and the two want opposite reactions. After an 8 the
       merge was never attempted, so re-running the whole command is safe. After a 4
       the merge WAS attempted and may have succeeded, so re-running risks a second
       landing; verify with --verify-only first.
       Measured 2026-08-25: this collapse made three race cases in
       gh-merge-verified-test pass on an exit 4 produced by the admission guard rather
       than by the race they claimed to exercise.
    9  REFUSED TO MERGE: every `passed-review-<lane>` label claimed on this head was
       bound by the SAME agent, so the two-lane rule was met in form and not in
       substance. Nothing was attempted. Scoped like 6: a pull request claiming fewer
       than two lanes is untouched, and a binding whose issuer is UNKNOWN makes this
       DECLINE TO ANSWER rather than refuse -- it refuses on evidence of one agent,
       never on the absence of evidence of two.

WHAT A CALLER SHOULD DO WITH EACH, BECAUSE A CODE IS ONLY USEFUL IF IT IMPLIES AN ACTION
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
An exit code shared by two conditions is a COLLAPSED VALUE: the caller cannot tell
whether ITS ENVIRONMENT is wrong or THE HEAD is, and those want opposite responses.
That was briefly true here -- a stale-TOOL refusal and a stale-LABEL refusal both
exited 6 -- and it is the same shape as a failed F_GETFL mapped to the same zero as
success. The codes are split now; the actions are written down so they stay split.

  RETRY IS REASONABLE -- the condition is about THIS RUN, not the pull request:
    3  re-run once. Usually the stale refs/pull/N/head race; if the verdict changes,
       the first answer was the race. Do NOT loop -- if it persists, read it by hand.
    4  fix the environment and re-run -- BUT the merge was already attempted, so
       verify with --verify-only before re-merging. It is the ABSENCE of an answer,
       never permission.
    8  fix API access and re-run the whole command. NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED, so a plain
       re-run is safe. This is the code to read when the admission check itself could
       not resolve.
    7  refresh the checkout, or run from a current one, then re-run. Nothing about the
       pull request is being questioned.

  DO NOT RETRY -- the condition is about the PULL REQUEST; re-running changes nothing:
    5  a reviewer refused THIS head. The head must move (push the fix) or the refusal
       must be withdrawn in the grammar. A second APPROVED-AT does not override it.
    6  the label is a stale cache of an approval earned at another sha. Re-request the
       lane at the CURRENT head. Removing the label to get past this is defeating the
       check, not satisfying it.

  NEITHER -- a human reads these before anything else happens:
    2  GitHub refused the merge: conflicts, protection, or a lost race.
    1  usage or ref resolution. The command was wrong, not the world.

THE LINE THAT MUST NOT BE CROSSED: 4 and 7 mean "this run could not establish
something"; 5 and 6 mean "the pull request is not admissible". A caller that retries a
5 is trying to outwait a reviewer. A caller that refuses on a 7 is blaming the pull
request for its own stale checkout.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import atexit
import json
import re
import pathlib
import os
import shutil
import tempfile
import time
import subprocess
import sys


# Scratch refs are namespaced PER PROCESS. A fixed name races: two agents running
# this concurrently in one repository both fetch into refs/gh-merge-verified/main,
# and git refuses the second with "incorrect old value provided" -- a
# compare-and-swap failure that --force does not prevent, because the ref moved
# between read and write rather than merely being non-fast-forward. Observed live
# on 2026-08-25 while verifying hermit#2521, where a legitimate landing came back
# COULD NOT DETERMINE. Exit 4 was correct and the tool was honest; it just could
# not do its job while a sibling held the ref.
SCRATCH = f"refs/gh-merge-verified/{os.getpid()}"
# Scratch refs live in both the repository being landed and this tool's own
# repository. Remember each explicitly: deleting `toolmain` from the caller's
# repository does not delete the ref that `git -C <tool-dir> fetch` created.
SCRATCH_REPOSITORIES = {os.getcwd()}

# How long to wait for `refs/heads/main` to advance after a merge this tool
# performed, and how often to re-ask. Small because the remote settles in
# well under a second in practice; the bound exists so an unresolved race
# terminates as COULD NOT DETERMINE rather than hanging.
# TEST-ONLY OVERRIDES, so the self-test can exercise the timeout without waiting
# the production bound. Production callers never set these.
MAIN_ADVANCE_TIMEOUT_S = float(os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_ADVANCE_TIMEOUT_S", "30"))
# Same bound, applied to `refs/pull/N/head` when the caller states which head
# they pushed. See the --expect-head handling for why that ref needs it too.
PR_HEAD_TIMEOUT_S = float(os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_PR_HEAD_TIMEOUT_S", "30"))
PR_HEAD_POLL_S = float(os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_PR_HEAD_POLL_S", "1"))
MAIN_ADVANCE_POLL_S = float(os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_ADVANCE_POLL_S", "1"))

# WHEN THE OLD-SCHEME REFS BECOME SAFE TO DELETE
#
# Before this namespacing, scratch refs were FIXED names -- refs/gh-merge-verified/main
# and refs/gh-merge-verified/<pr>. Sixteen of those were left in this repository on
# 2026-08-25 and were DELIBERATELY NOT removed at the time: deleting a ref in a shared
# repository can break a sibling that is between its fetch and its rev-parse, and this
# box runs a dozen agents.
#
# They are distinguishable from the current scheme by SHAPE, not by guesswork:
#     old scheme   refs/gh-merge-verified/<leaf>        exactly ONE component after the prefix
#     this scheme  refs/gh-merge-verified/<pid>/pr      exactly TWO
#
# THE CONDITION UNDER WHICH DELETING THEM IS SAFE, so the next tidy-up does not have to
# guess: nothing writes the one-component names any more, so the only hazard is a
# process still running a copy of this tool from BEFORE this change. That is checkable
# directly rather than inferred:
#
#     pgrep -f gh-merge-verified            # must return nothing
#     git for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' refs/gh-merge-verified \
#       | awk -F/ 'NF-2 == 1'               # exactly these, and only these
#     ... | xargs -r -n1 git update-ref -d
#
# With no verifier process running, no writer of those names exists and the deletion
# cannot race anything. With one running, wait -- a full run is seconds, not minutes.
# Do NOT delete two-component refs: those belong to a LIVE process by construction.


def run(args: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    """Run with NO shell and NO pipe, so the status is the command's own."""
    p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True)
    return p.returncode, p.stdout, p.stderr


def write_receipt(path, record: dict) -> None:
    """Write the landing receipt, or refuse to write one that overclaims.

    ⚠️ THE RECEIPT MUST NOT BE ABLE TO CLAIM MORE THAN THE RUN ESTABLISHED. A
    record saying `corroborated: true` while its two shas disagree is worse than
    no record: an audit reading it counts the run as checked, and the failure
    becomes undetectable in exactly the way this field exists to prevent. So the
    writer re-asserts the invariant it is about to persist, and dies 4 rather than
    emit a receipt whose own fields contradict each other.
    """
    if record.get("corroborated"):
        against = record.get("corroborated_against")
        resolved = record.get("resolved_head") or ""
        if not against:
            die(4, "gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — receipt claims corroboration "
                   "with nothing to corroborate against; refusing to write it.")
        if not (resolved.startswith(against) or against.startswith(resolved)):
            die(4, "gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — receipt would record a "
                   "CORROBORATED landing whose two shas disagree; refusing to write it.",
                f"  resolved_head        {resolved}",
                f"  corroborated_against {against}",
                "  A receipt claiming a check it did not pass is worse than no receipt:",
                "  an audit would count this run as verified.",
                "  This is NOT a statement about whether the change landed.")
    if not path:
        return
    try:
        with open(path, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(record, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
            fh.write("\n")
    except OSError as exc:
        die(4, f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — cannot write receipt {path}: {exc}",
            "  This is NOT a statement about whether the change landed.")
    print(f"gh-merge-verified: receipt written to {path}")


def die(code: int, *lines: str) -> None:
    for line in lines:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def cleanup_scratch_refs(prefix: str) -> None:
    """Delete one completed process's scratch refs from each repository used."""
    for repository in SCRATCH_REPOSITORIES:
        for suffix in ("pr", "main", "toolmain"):
            subprocess.run(
                ["git", "-C", repository, "update-ref", "-d", f"{prefix}/{suffix}"],
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
            )


def cleanup_scratch() -> None:
    """Delete this process's scratch refs. Leaking one ref per invocation would
    grow the ref namespace without bound in a repository many agents share."""
    cleanup_scratch_refs(SCRATCH)


def gh_argv(args: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Route `gh` through `with-proxy` where that wrapper exists.

    ⚠️ WRAPPED HERE RATHER THAN DOCUMENTED. On a proxy-required box a bare `gh`
    cannot reach api.github.com, so the merge dies with a network error before
    it has said anything about the pull request -- and a tool that emits
    "network is unreachable" reads as BROKEN rather than as correctly declining
    to act. The condition is not rare on these hosts: measured 2026-08-25,
    `git ls-remote` succeeded against github.com while `curl
    https://api.github.com` failed outright on the same machine.

    Telling every caller to remember a prefix is the kind of rule that depends
    on memory, which this file's neighbour argues is a preference rather than a
    fix. So the wrapper is applied internally, and only when it is actually
    present, so boxes without it are unaffected.
    """
    return ["with-proxy", *args] if shutil.which("with-proxy") else args


def looks_unreachable(text: str) -> bool:
    """Does this failure say the network was never crossed?

    Kept deliberately narrow. The point is to separate "GitHub REFUSED the
    merge" -- a verdict about the pull request -- from "we never reached
    GitHub", which is a statement about this box and must never be recorded as
    a refusal.
    """
    lowered = text.lower()
    return any(
        marker in lowered
        for marker in (
            "network is unreachable",
            "could not resolve host",
            "connection refused",
            "connection timed out",
            "dial tcp",
            "proxy",
            "certificate",
            "tls handshake",
            "i/o timeout",
        )
    )


def _load_grammar():
    """The marker grammar, from its single authority.

    ⚠️ CALL, DO NOT REIMPLEMENT. This file used to carry its own copy of the
    marker parsing. Two parsers for one grammar is the defect sweep check 14
    names, and these two HAD already drifted: the authority anchored at column 0
    and so failed open on a heading-formatted refusal, while this copy stripped
    decoration and did not. A landing guard and a review board are entitled to
    different POLICY -- everything below this function is policy, and it is
    deliberately stricter -- but they must not disagree about what a marker line
    SAYS.

    Imported by path rather than as a package because `ci-hub/health` is not one.
    If the authority cannot be loaded we do NOT silently fall back to a private
    parser: a landing guard that quietly downgrades its own grammar is the exact
    failure this refactor removes. The caller refuses instead.
    """
    import importlib.util

    target = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "health" / "approval_binding.py"
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("approval_binding", target)
    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
        raise RuntimeError(f"cannot load the marker grammar from {target}")
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    try:
        spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    except SystemExit:  # the authority guards its own CLI entry point
        pass
    return module


_SHA_IN_LINE = re.compile(r"\b([0-9a-f]{7,40})\b")
_LANE_IN_LINE = re.compile(r"\b(claude|codex)\b", re.IGNORECASE)

_REFUSAL_SHAPED = re.compile(r"CHANGES[-\s]?REQUESTED", re.IGNORECASE)

# ⚠️ REFUSAL-SHAPED **AND A LANE NAMED NEARBY**, which is what makes the over-block
# survivable. `_REFUSAL_SHAPED` alone matches the words "changes requested" in any
# prose, and while the pre-filter above skipped such lines for lacking the full
# 40-hex, that was an accident of the filter rather than a policy -- fixing the
# filter made the over-block reachable and it began firing on sentences ABOUT the
# convention. Measured over all 27 open pull requests on 2026-08-25:
#
#     token anywhere                 39 lines,  9 PRs   (1 pure-prose false positive)
#     token + lane within ~40 chars  26 lines,  8 PRs   (0 false positives)
#
# so requiring a lane costs nothing measurable and stops the guard blocking a pull
# request for explaining the rule -- which is how a guard gets switched off. This
# is the same rule ci-hub/bin/changes-requested-gate uses; two extractors with two
# policies is the divergence that produced this whole family of defects.
_REFUSAL_WITH_LANE = re.compile(
    r"CHANGES[-\s]?REQUESTED[^\n]{0,40}?\b(claude|codex)\b", re.IGNORECASE
)



def fetch_current_main_tool() -> tuple[pathlib.Path | None, str, str, str]:
    """Fetch this tool's main branch and resolve its script blob and commit.

    Returns ``(tool_dir, blob, main_commit, error)``. An error is a refusal to
    infer either value; callers decide whether to report it as a failed
    self-check or a failed request to run the current-main copy.
    """
    here = pathlib.Path(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    tool_dir = here.parent
    SCRATCH_REPOSITORIES.add(str(tool_dir))
    rel = "ci-hub/bin/gh-merge-verified"
    rc, tool_remote, _ = run(
        ["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "remote", "get-url", "origin"]
    )
    if rc != 0:
        return (
            None,
            "",
            "",
            "could not resolve the origin remote of this tool's own checkout",
        )
    rc, _, _ = run(
        [
            "git",
            "-C",
            str(tool_dir),
            "fetch",
            "--quiet",
            "--force",
            tool_remote.strip(),
            f"refs/heads/main:{SCRATCH}/toolmain",
        ]
    )
    if rc != 0:
        return None, "", "", "could not fetch dev-hermit main"
    rc, blob, _ = run(
        ["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/toolmain:{rel}"]
    )
    if rc != 0:
        return None, "", "", f"could not resolve {rel} on dev-hermit main"
    rc, main_commit, _ = run(
        ["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/toolmain"]
    )
    if rc != 0:
        return None, "", "", "could not resolve the fetched dev-hermit main commit"
    return tool_dir, blob.strip(), main_commit.strip(), ""


def this_tool_is_current() -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Is the copy of this file being executed the one on origin/main?

    WHY A LANDER MUST ASK THIS ABOUT ITSELF. The admission check below cannot
    protect anyone if the copy being run predates it -- and an OLD COPY CANNOT
    REFUSE, because the refusal is the very thing it lacks. Its landings look
    exactly like guarded ones: same exit 0, same VERIFIED line, same receipt.

    Measured 2026-08-25: the shared primary checkout at /home/newton/work/dev-hermit
    was 250 commits behind, and its gh-merge-verified was 374 lines with ZERO
    references to the guard while origin/main had 722 lines with it. SEVEN pull
    requests landed from there after the guard reached main, none of them checked.
    None happened to carry a refusal, so the realised harm was zero -- but the
    landings were clean by luck, not by admission, and nothing in their output
    distinguished the two.

    Refreshing the checkout fixes that until it goes stale again. THE DEFECT IS A
    GATE THAT SKIPS ITSELF WHEN ITS ENVIRONMENT IS WRONG, not the staleness, so
    this refuses instead of warning.

    Returns (is_current, detail). A failure to determine returns False: not being
    able to prove the guard is present is not permission to proceed without it.
    """
    # ⚠️ SCOPED TEST SEAM. The self-check is refused-by-default in production, but a
    # harness whose `gh` is a stub performs no real merge and cannot fetch dev-hermit.
    # This opt-out is honoured ONLY when the remote is a local path -- a fixture --
    # so it cannot be used to bypass the check against a real forge. Measured
    # 2026-08-25: without this, the self-check fired first and made three race cases
    # in gh-merge-verified-test unreachable, which is a guard breaking the test that
    # would have caught the guard.
    if os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_SELFCHECK") == "off":
        remote = os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_REMOTE", "")
        if remote and not remote.startswith(("http://", "https://", "git@", "ssh://")):
            return True, "self-check disabled for a local fixture remote"

    here = pathlib.Path(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    tool_dir, blob, _main_commit, error = fetch_current_main_tool()
    if error:
        return False, error
    assert tool_dir is not None
    rc, mine, _ = run(["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "hash-object", str(here)])
    if rc != 0:
        return False, "could not hash the running copy of this tool"
    if mine.strip() != blob:
        return False, (f"this copy is {mine.strip()[:12]}, dev-hermit main has "
                       f"{blob[:12]}")
    return True, "current"


def without_current_main_flag(argv: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Remove this tool's flag without touching arguments after ``--``."""
    result: list[str] = []
    own_options = True
    for arg in argv:
        if own_options and arg == "--run-current-main-copy":
            continue
        result.append(arg)
        if arg == "--":
            own_options = False
    return result


def execute_current_main_copy(argv: list[str]) -> None:
    """Run the exact script fetched from dev-hermit main.

    This function does not admit or merge anything. It writes the fetched blob
    beside the invoked script so the current-main copy can find the same
    ``ci-hub/health`` and ``ci-hub/lib`` dependencies, checks the written bytes,
    and starts that copy with this request flag removed. The fetched copy then
    performs its ordinary self-check and every ordinary admission check.
    """
    tool_dir, blob, main_commit, error = fetch_current_main_tool()
    if error:
        die(
            7,
            "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- could not obtain the current-main copy.",
            f"    {error}",
            "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. Refresh network access or the tool checkout, then re-run",
            "  the same command. Do not replace this with --verify-only: that checks only a merge",
            "  that already happened and performs no admission.",
        )
    assert tool_dir is not None

    temporary: pathlib.Path | None = None
    try:
        try:
            descriptor, raw_path = tempfile.mkstemp(
                prefix=".gh-merge-verified-current-main-", dir=tool_dir
            )
            temporary = pathlib.Path(raw_path)
        except OSError as exc:
            die(
                7,
                "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- cannot create the temporary current-main copy",
                f"  beside {__file__}: {exc}",
                "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. Make this checkout writable, then re-run the same command.",
            )
        try:
            with os.fdopen(descriptor, "wb") as output:
                materialize = subprocess.run(
                    ["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "cat-file", "blob", blob],
                    stdout=output,
                    stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                )
            os.chmod(temporary, 0o700)
        except OSError as exc:
            die(
                7,
                "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- could not write the current-main copy",
                f"  beside {__file__}: {exc}",
                "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. Repair the checkout or its permissions, then re-run.",
            )
        if materialize.returncode != 0:
            detail = materialize.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()
            die(
                7,
                "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- fetched main but could not read its verifier",
                f"  blob {blob[:12]}: {detail}",
                "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. Repair the git object store or fetch, then re-run.",
            )
        rc, written, _ = run(
            ["git", "-C", str(tool_dir), "hash-object", str(temporary)]
        )
        if rc != 0 or written.strip() != blob:
            die(
                7,
                "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- the written current-main copy does not match",
                f"  the fetched blob {blob[:12]}; NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED.",
                "  Remove the incomplete temporary file if it remains, repair the checkout, and re-run.",
            )
        print(
            "gh-merge-verified: running the byte-identical verifier from dev-hermit main "
            f"{main_commit[:12]} ({blob[:12]}).",
            flush=True,
        )
        print(
            "  The modified working-tree copy is not admitting or merging this pull request.",
            flush=True,
        )
        try:
            child = subprocess.Popen(
                [sys.executable, str(temporary), *without_current_main_flag(argv)]
            )
        except OSError as exc:
            die(
                7,
                "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- could not start the current-main copy",
                f"    {exc}",
                "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. Repair the Python executable or checkout, then re-run.",
            )
        returncode = child.wait()
        # The first invocation after this change runs the previous main copy,
        # whose cleanup does not yet know that `toolmain` lives in the tool
        # repository. Its PID makes those refs exact and safe to remove after it
        # exits; no broad ref scan or guess about another live process is needed.
        cleanup_scratch_refs(f"refs/gh-merge-verified/{child.pid}")
        code = returncode if returncode >= 0 else 128 - returncode
        raise SystemExit(code)
    finally:
        if temporary is not None:
            try:
                temporary.unlink()
            except FileNotFoundError:
                pass
            except OSError as exc:
                print(
                    f"gh-merge-verified: warning: could not remove temporary current-main copy "
                    f"{temporary}: {exc}",
                    "  Remove that named file manually; it is no longer used by this run.",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )


def blocking_changes_requests(pr: str, repo: str, head: str) -> list[str]:
    """Anchored refusals that name THIS head and have not been withdrawn.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. A lander that greps for `APPROVED-AT` and merges when it
    finds one is looking only for good news: it cannot see a refusal, so it
    merges over one regardless of what any board says. Measured 2026-08-25 on
    https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2591 -- `CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude
    <head>` was posted in the anchored grammar at the exact head, and the merge
    went through 3m42s later because the check never asked. Two defects reached
    main, one a recipe that prints MERGES-CLEAN about a base it never fetched.

    Of the 184 pull requests merged that day, seven carried such a marker; five
    named an EARLIER sha, which is the correct clearing path. TWO named their
    merged head: pull/2434 and pull/2591.

    THE RULE IS EXACT-HEAD AND FAILS CLOSED:
      * a refusal naming this head blocks even if an APPROVED-AT also names it.
        Two reviewers disagreeing is not settled by whoever posted last -- that
        is a coin toss -- so this stops and makes a human choose.
      * pushing the fix moves the sha, so the refusal stops naming the head.
        That is the ordinary way through and needs no override.
      * CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT at the same sha also clears it.
    There is deliberately no --force. An override in a landing path is what
    somebody in a hurry reaches for, and somebody in a hurry is the failure.

    SHARP EDGE, AND IT IS THE SAFE DIRECTION: leading whitespace, blockquote
    markers and markdown emphasis are STRIPPED before matching, so a refusal
    written as a heading or in bold still blocks -- and so does a quotation of
    one. An earlier version matched only column 0 and FAILED OPEN on every
    decorated marker; agent(hermit-003) found the same fail-open in the board
    parser, where a heading-formatted refusal read as APPROVED. For a landing
    guard a false negative merges over a block while a false positive costs one
    push, so this over-blocks on purpose.
    """
    rc, out, _ = run(gh_argv(["gh", "pr", "view", pr, "--repo", repo,
                              "--json", "comments,reviews"]))
    if rc != 0:
        return ["__UNREADABLE__"]
    try:
        doc = json.loads(out)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return ["__UNREADABLE__"]

    bodies = [c.get("body") or "" for c in doc.get("comments", [])]
    bodies += [r.get("body") or "" for r in doc.get("reviews", [])]
    native = [r for r in doc.get("reviews", [])
              if (r.get("state") or "") == "CHANGES_REQUESTED"]

    grammar = _load_grammar()

    # ⚠️ WITHDRAWALS ARE TRACKED PER LANE. Blocking stays lane-agnostic (see the
    # policy note below) because that direction fails CLOSED; clearing must not,
    # or one family discharges another's refusal. Reproduced live on hermit#2613.
    blocking, withdrawn = [], set()
    # (kind, lane, sha) already accepted, oldest first. A verdict is issued once;
    # a later copy of it is a CITATION and must not block again.
    #
    # ⚠️ THIS CHANGES NOTHING TODAY, AND THAT IS STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED.
    # Measured 2026-08-25 on the cases that break the gate -- hermit#2615's five
    # markers for two verdicts, and a reviewer citing their own answered refusal
    # while approving -- this path already returned the right answer, because its
    # near-miss check is `_REFUSAL_NEARMISS.match`, anchored, so a mid-line
    # citation never matched it at all. The gate scans the whole line and does not
    # have that accidental immunity.
    #
    # It is here because the immunity IS accidental. This net was once "the token
    # ANYWHERE in the line" and was narrowed and restored across ff2a3d83/7db64676;
    # if it is ever widened again this path acquires the citation defect instantly,
    # and one landing path fixed while the other is not is a divergence this file's
    # own comments record happening twice.
    issued: list[tuple[str | None, str, str | None, str | None]] = []
    # ⚠️ ELIGIBILITY COMES FROM THE SHARED GRAMMAR, so a line that cannot bind an
    # approval cannot trigger a refusal here either. This function's fail-closed
    # fallback matches a refusal-shaped line ANYWHERE, which is the right policy
    # for a landing guard and the wrong thing to point at a fenced example: it
    # refused 6 of the 12 heads it blocked on 2026-08-25, and because those lines
    # name no head, pushing never cleared them.
    eligible = grammar.marker_lines
    for body in bodies:
        _author = grammar.comment_author(body or "")
        for line in eligible(body):
            # ⚠️ THIS PRE-FILTER USED TO READ `if not head or head not in line`,
            # AND IT MADE THE FAIL-CLOSED BRANCH BELOW UNREACHABLE. Requiring the
            # full 40-hex in the raw line skipped exactly the two shapes the
            # over-block was written for, before they could reach it. Measured
            # 2026-08-25 by calling this function directly:
            #
            #   CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude b3ca4e15        -> []  NOT BLOCKED
            #   CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude — see above     -> []  NOT BLOCKED
            #   CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: claude <full 40-hex>   -> blocked
            #
            # Abbreviated shas are normal in these markers -- hermit#2176's own
            # refusal is written `b3ca4e15…` -- so the common form was invisible
            # to the guard while the docstring said it over-blocked. And `not head`
            # skipped EVERY line when the head could not be resolved, which is the
            # reassuring direction: unable to check read as nothing to report.
            #
            # Now: a refusal-shaped line is always evaluated. Binding is decided
            # below, where an abbreviated sha can be prefix-matched and a missing
            # one can fail closed.
            if not (_REFUSAL_WITH_LANE.search(line) or (head and head in line)):
                continue
            parsed = grammar.parse_marker(line)
            if parsed is not None:
                kind, _lane, sha = parsed
                # Bind by PREFIX in both directions: markers are routinely written
                # with an abbreviated sha, and an exact-string compare against the
                # 40-hex silently treats `b3ca4e15` as naming some other commit.
                if not (sha and head and (head.startswith(sha) or sha.startswith(head))):
                    if kind == grammar.MARKER_REFUSED and not sha:
                        # A refusal we cannot bind is not a refusal that does not
                        # apply. Fail closed, same choice as the unparseable case.
                        # Lane None: no withdrawal can prove it owns this one.
                        blocking.append((None, line.strip()))
                    continue
                # POLICY: BLOCKING is deliberately lane-agnostic -- a refusal in
                # ANY lane stops this merge, and a lander only needs to know
                # whether anybody said no.
                #
                # ⚠️ CLEARING IS NOT, AND THAT ASYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE POINT. This
                # code destructured the lane and threw it away, so ANY lane's
                # withdrawal discharged EVERY lane's refusal. Lane-agnostic
                # blocking fails CLOSED and is right; lane-agnostic clearing fails
                # OPEN and let one family retract another's block. Measured on
                # hermit#2613: a claude withdrawal cleared an unwithdrawn codex
                # refusal and both landers reported the head ready.
                #
                # A withdrawal that names no lane clears NOTHING -- it cannot say
                # whose refusal it discharges, and honouring it hands the same
                # unilateral clear back through the laneless spelling.
                if kind == grammar.MARKER_WITHDRAWN:
                    # ⚠️ AND BY ITS ISSUER, NOT ONLY ITS LANE. Lane-only clearing
                    # has exactly the shape the note above rejects, one level
                    # down: a lane is staffed by SEVERAL AGENTS, so agent B's
                    # withdrawal discharged agent A's refusal in the same lane.
                    # Measured 2026-08-26 by driving this function directly --
                    # a hermit-007 refusal plus a hermit-005 withdrawal returned
                    # blocking=0. Same defect as hermit#2613's cross-lane clear,
                    # same fail-open direction, one scope narrower.
                    #
                    # An UNSIGNED withdrawal clears NOTHING, for the reason the
                    # laneless one does not: it cannot say whose refusal it
                    # discharges, and honouring it hands the unilateral clear
                    # straight back through the unsigned spelling.
                    _w = grammar.marker_issuer(line) or _author
                    if _lane and _w:
                        withdrawn.add((str(_lane).lower(), _w))
                elif kind == grammar.MARKER_REFUSED:
                    _l = str(_lane).lower() if _lane else None
                    if grammar.is_citation(issued, _author, kind, _l, sha):
                        continue
                    issued.append((_author, kind, _l, sha))
                    blocking.append((_l, grammar.marker_issuer(line) or _author,
                                     line.strip()))
                continue
            # POLICY: over-block on a refusal-shaped line the grammar cannot
            # parse. For a landing guard a false negative merges over a block
            # while a false positive costs one push, so an unparseable refusal
            # naming this head is treated as a refusal. This is the same
            # fail-closed choice the authority now makes at its gate, expressed
            # here as blocking rather than as a non-zero exit.
            # ⚠️ CONVERGED ONTO THE SHARED RULE. This used to be a local regex
            # matching the token ANYWHERE in the line, which is why a sentence
            # discussing another pull request's refusal blocked a merge here while
            # changes-requested-gate allowed it. One definition now, so the two
            # landers cannot disagree about what is even refusal-shaped.
            if grammar.refusal_shaped(line):
                # ⚠️ EXCEPT A TEMPLATE. `CHANGES-REQUESTED-AT: <lane> <sha>` in a
                # comment teaching the grammar is documentation, and it carries no
                # sha -- so the ordinary clearing path (push, the sha moves, the
                # refusal stops naming the head) can NEVER clear it. Blocking on it
                # is a permanent refusal of a head nobody objected to.
                # `changes-requested-gate` already skipped it at 32faa7e2 while this
                # path still refused it: one rule, two homes, immediate divergence.
                # Now shared, so they cannot disagree again.
                if grammar.is_template(line):
                    continue
                # ⚠️ AND A NEAR-MISS REFUSAL THAT NAMES A SUPERSEDED SHA HAS ALREADY
                # BEEN CLEARED THE ORDINARY WAY. This branch used to block on any
                # refusal-shaped line whatever sha it named, so
                # `CHANGES-REQUESTED: claude <old-sha>` refused every future head
                # FOREVER -- the author pushes the fix, the sha moves, and the line
                # still blocks because nothing ever compared it to the head.
                # `changes-requested-gate` extracts the sha and binds by prefix;
                # this now does the same, so the two agree and the ordinary clearing
                # path works for the near-miss family too.
                #
                # A refusal with NO readable sha still blocks: unbindable is not
                # inapplicable, which is the fail-closed choice this branch exists
                # to make.
                # ⚠️ NEITHER A LANE NOR A SHA MEANS PROSE, NOT A VERDICT. The
                # near-miss grammar is case-insensitive and allows a space, so an
                # ordinary sentence opening a review -- "Changes requested, on the
                # deleted value pin alone." -- is refusal-shaped and was blocking
                # here with no sha to ever stop naming the head. Pushing could not
                # clear it. Same defect the sibling lander had at
                # changes-requested-gate; fixed in both, with the same
                # discriminator, so they cannot drift.
                #
                # Somebody asserting a verdict supplies a lane or a sha; somebody
                # writing a sentence supplies neither. This narrows what counts as
                # a refusal ATTEMPT and leaves the withdrawal path alone --
                # widening withdrawal would let an accidental sentence CLEAR a
                # real refusal, which is the dangerous direction.
                if (
                    _SHA_IN_LINE.search(line) is None
                    and _LANE_IN_LINE.search(line) is None
                ):
                    continue
                near_sha = _SHA_IN_LINE.search(line)
                if near_sha and head and not (
                    head.startswith(near_sha.group(1))
                    or near_sha.group(1).startswith(head)
                ):
                    continue
                # Unparseable: the lane is unknown, so no withdrawal clears it.
                _nsha = near_sha.group(1) if near_sha else None
                if grammar.is_citation(issued, _author, grammar.MARKER_REFUSED, None, _nsha):
                    continue
                issued.append((_author, grammar.MARKER_REFUSED, None, _nsha))
                blocking.append((None, _author, line.strip()))
    # ⚠️ EACH REFUSAL IS CLEARED ONLY BY ITS OWN LANE **AND ITS OWN ISSUER**. A
    # refusal whose lane or issuer could not be read is cleared by nobody -- its
    # owner is unknown, so no withdrawal can prove it owns it. All of them still
    # clear the ordinary way: the author pushes and the sha stops naming the head.
    live = [text for lane, issuer, text in blocking
            if lane is None or issuer is None
            or (lane, issuer) not in withdrawn]
    live += ["(GitHub review state CHANGES_REQUESTED)" for _ in native]
    return live


def stale_review_labels(
    pr: str, repo: str, head: str, single_issuer: "list[str] | None" = None
) -> list[str]:
    """`passed-review-<lane>` labels whose lane is NOT bound to THIS head.

    ⚠️ A LABEL OUTLIVES THE SHA IT WAS EARNED AT. That is the whole defect. The
    approval was genuine; the head then moved and the label kept saying yes.
    Measured on https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2601, merged
    2026-08-25T18:57:53Z with authoritative=0: the codex lane bound at 94d9a865,
    hermit-012 pushed a rebase to 1f4982f7 which voids the lane, and the claude
    reviewer posted `APPROVED-AT 1f4982f7...` with NO LANE TOKEN so it parsed as
    malformed rather than as a lane. Comment evidence was therefore empty -- and
    the pre-rebase `passed-review-codex` label admitted the merge anyway.

    THIS IS THE DANGEROUS INVERSION of the stranded-approval failure. When a
    rebase strands approvals the binding is LOST: work stalls, visibly, and
    somebody re-reviews. Here the binding is STALE: the merge goes through and
    nothing says so. Same mechanism, opposite direction, and only one of them is
    safe to get wrong.

    SCOPE, deliberately narrow. This does NOT impose a review requirement on
    pull requests that never claimed one -- a pull request with no
    `passed-review-*` label is untouched. It refuses only when a label ASSERTS an
    approval that the evidence no longer supports at this head. The label is a
    cache; this makes the cache unable to outlive what it caches.

    Returns the human-readable rows to print, or ``["__UNREADABLE__"]`` if the
    state could not be established -- which is refused as an absence of an
    answer, never treated as permission.

    ⚠️ `single_issuer` IS AN OUT-PARAMETER, in the style of `malformed` in the
    grammar module: when supplied, it receives the rows describing lanes that are
    all BOUND AT THIS HEAD BY ONE AGENT. It is computed here rather than in a
    second function because the answer needs the same `gh pr view` payload, and a
    second call would be a second query -- this fleet has tripped GitHub's
    secondary rate limit by asking the same question twice, after which every
    gate reads INDETERMINATE and nothing can land at all.

    THE LANE IS NOT THE REVIEWER. `claude` and `codex` name a HARNESS FAMILY, and
    nineteen agents share those two names, so two lanes at one head has never been
    evidence that two reviewers looked at it -- one agent can bind both and the
    record is indistinguishable from independent review. dev-hermit#191 made the
    binding carry its issuer so this function can finally ask.
    """
    grammar = _load_grammar()
    rc, out, _ = run(gh_argv(["gh", "pr", "view", pr, "--repo", repo,
                              "--json", "labels,comments,reviews"]))
    if rc != 0:
        return ["__UNREADABLE__"]
    try:
        doc = json.loads(out)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return ["__UNREADABLE__"]

    labelled = {
        (l.get("name") or "").rsplit("-", 1)[-1].lower()
        for l in doc.get("labels", [])
        if (l.get("name") or "").startswith("passed-review-")
    }
    if not labelled:
        return []

    comments = list(doc.get("comments", [])) + list(doc.get("reviews", []))
    stale = []
    # lane -> the agents that bound THIS head on it. `None` in the set means a
    # binding whose issuer neither the marker nor the comment's disclosure tag
    # stated.
    bound_by: dict[str, set] = {}
    for lane in sorted(labelled):
        if lane not in grammar.LANES:
            continue
        issuers: list = []
        shas = grammar.lane_shas(comments, lane, [], issuers=issuers)
        state = grammar.classify_approval(head, shas)
        if state != grammar.BOUND_CURRENT:
            bound = shas[-1][:12] if shas else "nothing"
            stale.append(
                f"passed-review-{lane}: {state} -- newest {lane} binding is {bound}, "
                f"head is {head[:12]}"
            )
            continue
        # Only bindings AT THIS HEAD count. A lane's earlier binding on a
        # superseded sha says nothing about who reviewed the code being merged.
        bound_by[lane] = {i for sha, i in issuers if sha.lower() == head.lower()}

    if single_issuer is not None and len(bound_by) >= 2:
        agents = set().union(*bound_by.values())
        # ⚠️ UNATTRIBUTABLE IS NOT "THE SAME AGENT", AND IT IS NOT "TWO AGENTS".
        # Nothing emits `BY` yet, so the issuer usually comes from the comment's
        # disclosure tag -- and that tag is missing on a large minority of agent
        # comments. Refusing on an unknown identity would block nearly every pull
        # request in the fleet on the day this lands, for a fact never
        # established. So an absent issuer makes this check DECLINE TO ANSWER for
        # that head, which is the same discipline as the surrounding scope rule:
        # refuse on evidence, never on the absence of it.
        if None not in agents and len(agents) == 1:
            who = next(iter(agents))
            single_issuer.append(
                f"{', '.join(sorted(bound_by))}: all bound at {head[:12]} by "
                f"ONE agent -- {who}"
            )
    return stale


def parse_options(argv: list[str]):
    """Split this tool's own options from the `gh pr merge` passthrough.

    ⚠️ POSITION MUST NOT MATTER FOR A FLAG THIS TOOL OWNS, AND A FLAG MUST NEVER BE
    LOST. Both halves are measured failures, not hypotheticals.

    The separator used to REPLACE the accumulated passthrough
    (`extra = argv[i+1:]`) rather than extend it, so anything collected before `--`
    was silently discarded. On a real landing that dropped `--expect-head <sha>`:
    the invoked copy predated the flag, so it fell through to the passthrough, and
    `--` then overwrote it. The merge SUCCEEDED, `gh` saw valid flags, and the head
    was never corroborated -- a guard reported as used that had not run.

    Unrecognised flags before the separator are still FORWARDED to `gh`, and that
    is deliberate: `gh-merge-verified <pr> --repo <r> --squash --delete-branch`
    without any `--` is the documented and widely used form. Refusing them would
    have caught the typo case at the cost of breaking every existing caller -- so
    the ordering is made harmless rather than policed.
    """
    repo = "rrnewton/hermit"
    verify_only = False
    use_current_main_copy = False
    expect_head = None
    receipt_path = None
    extra: list[str] = []
    i = 1
    while i < len(argv):
        arg = argv[i]
        if arg == "--repo":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise ValueError("--repo needs OWNER/REPO; run --help for an example")
            repo, i = argv[i + 1], i + 2
        elif arg == "--verify-only":
            verify_only, i = True, i + 1
        elif arg == "--run-current-main-copy":
            use_current_main_copy, i = True, i + 1
        elif arg == "--expect-head":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise ValueError("--expect-head needs the exact 40-hex PR head; run --help for an example")
            expect_head, i = argv[i + 1], i + 2
        elif arg == "--receipt":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                raise ValueError("--receipt needs an output path; run --help for an example")
            receipt_path, i = argv[i + 1], i + 2
        elif arg == "--":
            extra.extend(argv[i + 1 :])
            i = len(argv)
        else:
            extra.append(arg)
            i += 1
    return repo, verify_only, use_current_main_copy, expect_head, receipt_path, extra


def main() -> None:
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if argv and argv[0] in {"help", "-h", "--help"}:
        print(__doc__ or "")
        return
    if not argv:
        die(
            1,
            "gh-merge-verified: missing pull request number.",
            "  Pass the PR number first; run --help for examples and exit-code meanings.",
        )
    atexit.register(cleanup_scratch)

    pr = argv[0]
    if not pr.isdigit():
        die(
            1,
            f"gh-merge-verified: first argument must be a pull request number, got {pr!r}.",
            "  Pass the numeric PR number first; run --help for examples.",
        )

    try:
        (
            repo,
            verify_only,
            use_current_main_copy,
            expect_head,
            receipt_path,
            extra,
        ) = parse_options(argv)
    except ValueError as error:
        die(1, f"gh-merge-verified: {error}")

    if use_current_main_copy and verify_only:
        die(
            1,
            "gh-merge-verified: choose either --run-current-main-copy or --verify-only, not both.",
            "  --run-current-main-copy obtains the reviewed verifier so it can perform admission",
            "  before a merge.",
            "  --verify-only performs no admission; it checks content after a merge. If the merge",
            "  already happened, remove --run-current-main-copy.",
        )
    if use_current_main_copy:
        execute_current_main_copy(argv)

    # TEST-ONLY ESCAPE HATCH. The self-test needs to drive this against fixture
    # repositories on disk, because a verifier that has only ever been run
    # against a merge that SUCCEEDED has not been shown to refuse one that did
    # not. Production callers never set this; if it is unset the URL is built
    # from --repo exactly as before.
    remote_url = os.environ.get("GH_MERGE_VERIFIED_REMOTE") or f"https://github.com/{repo}.git"

    # Resolve the pull request head from the REMOTE. Doing this first means a
    # merge is never attempted against a number we cannot even read.
    # ⚠️ `refs/pull/N/head` READS STALE AFTER A PUSH. GitHub updates the branch ref
    # first and the pull-request ref a moment later, so reading it ONCE right after
    # pushing can hand back the PREVIOUS head. Everything downstream is then
    # computed from the wrong commit: the merge base, the file list, and every
    # ARRIVED/ABSENT verdict. The tool reports VERIFIED over the old change.
    #
    # Measured tonight as a second independent instance of one root cause -- reading
    # a moving reference once and trusting the answer -- alongside the post-merge
    # `refs/heads/main` race fixed above.
    #
    # `--expect-head` is how a caller says "I just pushed THIS". When given, the ref
    # is polled until it converges on that sha; if it never does, that is COULD NOT
    # DETERMINE, never a verdict about the change. When absent the tool cannot know
    # whether the ref has settled, so it says so rather than implying it checked.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT resolved by asking the API: verification here is pure git,
    # which is why this tool was the one kept, and an --expect-head the caller
    # already knows costs no network at all.
    if expect_head:
        pr_deadline = time.monotonic() + PR_HEAD_TIMEOUT_S
        while True:
            rc, out, err = run(["git", "fetch", "--quiet", "--force", remote_url,
                                f"refs/pull/{pr}/head:{SCRATCH}/pr"])
            if rc != 0:
                break
            rc_h, seen, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/pr"])
            seen = seen.strip()
            if rc_h == 0 and seen.startswith(expect_head) or expect_head.startswith(seen or "x"):
                break
            if time.monotonic() >= pr_deadline:
                die(4,
                    f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — refs/pull/{pr}/head is "
                    f"{seen[:12]} but --expect-head said {expect_head[:12]}, after "
                    f"{PR_HEAD_TIMEOUT_S}s.",
                    "  THIS IS NOT A STATEMENT ABOUT THE CHANGE. The pull-request ref lags a"
                    " push; if you did not just push, the sha you expected is simply wrong.")
            time.sleep(PR_HEAD_POLL_S)

    rc, out, err = run(["git", "fetch", "--quiet", "--force", remote_url,
                        f"refs/pull/{pr}/head:{SCRATCH}/pr"])
    if rc != 0:
        die(4, f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — cannot fetch refs/pull/{pr}/head from {repo}",
            "  This is NOT a statement about whether the change landed.",
            err.strip())
    rc, head, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/pr"])
    if not expect_head:
        # Not a refusal: the tool has no way to know whether the ref has settled,
        # so it discloses that rather than implying it checked. Silence here would
        # be the same defect as an empty check reading as a pass.
        print(f"gh-merge-verified: pr head taken from refs/pull/{pr}/head WITHOUT "
              "corroboration; that ref lags a push. Pass --expect-head <sha> if you "
              "just pushed.")
    head = head.strip()

    # ADMISSION, BEFORE ANY MERGE IS ATTEMPTED. Refuses rather than warns: a
    # warning printed by a landing path is read AFTER the merge.
    if not verify_only:
        # ⚠️ THE GUARD MUST PROVE IT IS THE CURRENT GUARD BEFORE IT VOUCHES FOR
        # ANYTHING. A stale copy silently has no admission check at all.
        current, detail = this_tool_is_current()
        if not current:
            die(7, "gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE -- this tool cannot prove it is "
                   "the current version, so its admission check may not exist in this copy.",
                f"    {detail}",
                "",
                "  A stale lander does not fail; it SKIPS the changes-request check and "
                "reports a",
                "  clean landing. That is indistinguishable from a checked one, which is why "
                "this",
                "  refuses rather than warning.",
                "",
                "  Run from a fresh checkout, or refresh this one (`make checkout-fresh` in "
                "dev-hermit).",
                "  If the reviewed pull request changes this file, pass "
                "--run-current-main-copy;",
                "  the modified copy will fetch and start the exact verifier from main, and only",
                "  that reviewed copy will perform admission and the merge.",
                "  Pass --verify-only only to prove an already-performed merge; it performs no",
                "  admission and is not a route for landing a change to this file.")
        blocking = blocking_changes_requests(pr, repo, head)
        if blocking == ["__UNREADABLE__"]:
            die(8, "gh-merge-verified: ADMISSION COULD NOT RUN -- could not read comments/reviews "
                   f"for #{pr}, so a changes-request at this head cannot be ruled out.",
                "  NOTHING WAS ATTEMPTED. This is not permission; it is the absence of an answer.",
                "  Distinct from exit 4 on purpose: 4 means the MERGE outcome is undetermined and "
                "something",
                "  may have happened. 8 means the CHECK never ran and nothing did.")
        if blocking:
            die(5, f"gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE #{pr} -- a changes-request names this "
                   f"exact head {head[:12]}.",
                *[f"    {b}" for b in blocking],
                "",
                "  An APPROVED-AT at the same head does NOT override this.",
                "  TO PROCEED: push the fix (the sha moves and the refusal stops naming the head),",
                f"  or have the reviewer post CHANGES-REQUESTED-WITHDRAWN-AT: <family> {head}",
                "  There is deliberately no override flag.",
                "  FALSE POSITIVE? The match is anchored to column 0, so an unindented quotation",
                "  of a marker re-asserts it. Indent quoted markers by one space.")
        one_agent: list[str] = []
        stale = stale_review_labels(pr, repo, head, single_issuer=one_agent)
        if stale == ["__UNREADABLE__"]:
            die(8, "gh-merge-verified: ADMISSION COULD NOT RUN -- could not read labels/comments "
                   f"for #{pr}, so a stale review label cannot be ruled out.",
                "  Nothing was merged. This is not permission; it is the absence of an answer.")
        if stale:
            die(6, f"gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE #{pr} -- a passed-review label asserts "
                   f"an approval that is not bound to this head {head[:12]}.",
                *[f"    {s}" for s in stale],
                "",
                "  The label is a CACHE of a review comment; a push voids the review but not the",
                "  label, so the cache outlives what it caches. This refuses on the evidence",
                "  rather than on the cache.",
                "  TO PROCEED: have the lane re-post APPROVED-AT: <lane> "
                f"{head} at this head,",
                "  or remove the stale label if the approval is genuinely no longer claimed.",
                "  ⚠️ A MISSING LANE TOKEN READS AS MALFORMED, NOT AS AN APPROVAL: write",
                "  'APPROVED-AT: claude <sha>', not 'APPROVED-AT <sha>'. That one-token typo is",
                "  what left #2601 with zero authoritative lanes while looking reviewed.")
        if one_agent:
            die(9, f"gh-merge-verified: REFUSING TO MERGE #{pr} -- every review lane claimed on "
                   f"this head was bound by the SAME agent.",
                *[f"    {s}" for s in one_agent],
                "",
                "  THE LANE IS NOT THE REVIEWER. 'claude' and 'codex' name a harness FAMILY,",
                "  and this fleet shares two lane names across nineteen agents, so two lanes",
                "  is not two reviewers. The two-lane rule exists to get two INDEPENDENT",
                "  judgements; one agent binding both satisfies its letter and none of it.",
                "  TO PROCEED: have a DIFFERENT agent review and post",
                f"  'APPROVED-AT: <lane> {head} BY <their-agent-id>' at this head.",
                "  Removing a label is not a remedy -- it drops the claim rather than",
                "  meeting it, and lands the same unreviewed code with less evidence.",
                "  ⚠️ SCOPE: this fires only when EVERY claimed lane resolves to ONE KNOWN",
                "  agent. An unattributable binding -- no 'BY' and no disclosure tag --",
                "  makes this check decline to answer rather than refuse.")
    if rc != 0 or not head:
        die(4, f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — cannot resolve the head of PR {pr}")

    # ⚠️ STAMPED HERE, AT RESOLVE TIME, NOT RECONSTRUCTED AT REPORT TIME.
    # TWO SHAS THAT AGREE ARE EVIDENCE; ONE SHA IS A NOTE. Recording only the head
    # we resolved leaves an auditor unable to separate a correct resolution from a
    # stale one, which is exactly why the pull-ref audit could report no more than
    # "clean as far as recorded". What makes the record load-bearing is the PAIR:
    # what we resolved, and what we checked it against.
    #
    # AND IT IS WRITTEN WHERE IT IS LEARNED. A field derived later from whatever the
    # process still happens to hold is the same guess with better manners -- the
    # explicit lesson from the drain-publication regex, which rebuilt at report time
    # a value it could have stamped at the moment it was true.
    resolution = {
        "resolved_head": head,
        "resolved_from": f"refs/pull/{pr}/head",
        "corroborated_against": expect_head or None,
        "corroborated": bool(expect_head),
    }
    if expect_head:
        # Asserted, not inferred from the absence of a warning. Silence reading as
        # "corroborated" is the same defect as an empty check reading as a pass.
        print(f"gh-merge-verified: pr head {head[:12]} CORROBORATED against "
              f"--expect-head {expect_head[:12]}")

    # ⚠️ CAPTURE MAIN BEFORE THE MERGE, so the verification can tell "the ref has
    # not moved yet" from "the change is not there". Without this the two are
    # indistinguishable and the race below reads as a verdict.
    main_before = None
    if not verify_only:
        rc_b, _, _ = run(["git", "fetch", "--quiet", "--force", remote_url,
                          f"refs/heads/main:{SCRATCH}/main"])
        if rc_b == 0:
            rc_b, mb, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/main"])
            if rc_b == 0:
                main_before = mb.strip()

    if not verify_only:
        # NO PIPE: the status read here is `gh pr merge`'s own.
        rc, out, err = run(gh_argv(["gh", "pr", "merge", pr, "--repo", repo, *extra]))
        sys.stdout.write(out)
        sys.stderr.write(err)
        if rc != 0:
            # ⚠️ NEVER RECORD "we could not reach GitHub" AS "GitHub refused the
            # merge". The first is about this box and says nothing about the
            # pull request; the second is a verdict. Collapsing them is how an
            # environment fault gets written down as a decision.
            if looks_unreachable(out + err):
                die(4, "", f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — the merge was never attempted "
                    f"(exit {rc}): the GitHub API was not reachable from this host.",
                    "  THIS IS NOT A STATEMENT ABOUT THE PULL REQUEST. Nothing was merged and "
                    "nothing was verified.",
                    "  `with-proxy` was " + ("used" if shutil.which("with-proxy") else "NOT FOUND on PATH")
                    + "; on a proxy-required box install or expose it, or run the merge from a host "
                      "that can reach api.github.com.")
            die(2, "", f"gh-merge-verified: the merge command FAILED (exit {rc}). Nothing was verified.",
                "  This is the case a piped exit status hides.")

    # Ask the remote what main holds now.
    #
    # ⚠️ THE CHECK CAN OUTRUN THE MERGE, AND THE FALSE VERDICT IS THE WORST ONE.
    # A single unguarded fetch here returns the PRE-MERGE main whenever GitHub has
    # not yet advanced `refs/heads/main`. Every file then reads as ABSENT and the
    # tool exits 3 — NOT LANDED — for a merge that succeeded. rc=3 is this tool's
    # strongest claim ("main still holds the pre-change content"), so an agent that
    # believes it re-lands work already on main or reopens a closed pull request.
    #
    # POLLING THE REF DOES NOT SOFTEN THE CONTENT CHECK. A successful merge MUST
    # advance main past the sha captured before it, so waiting for that one fact
    # removes the race without relaxing anything the comparison asserts.
    #
    # AND AN UNRESOLVED RACE IS `COULD NOT DETERMINE`, NEVER `NOT LANDED`. If main
    # never advances within the bound we exit 4, which this file's own contract
    # already defines as "the check did not complete ... so a broken verifier is
    # never read as a clean landing". Reporting 3 there would be asserting a fact
    # about the pull request from a fact about our timing.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + MAIN_ADVANCE_TIMEOUT_S
    waited = False
    while True:
        rc, out, err = run(["git", "fetch", "--quiet", "--force", remote_url,
                            f"refs/heads/main:{SCRATCH}/main"])
        if rc != 0:
            die(4, "gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — cannot fetch main", err.strip())
        rc, main_sha, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{SCRATCH}/main"])
        main_sha = main_sha.strip()
        if main_before is None or main_sha != main_before:
            break
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            die(4,
                f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — the merge reported success but "
                f"{remote_url} refs/heads/main has not moved from {main_before[:12]} after "
                f"{MAIN_ADVANCE_TIMEOUT_S}s.",
                "  THIS IS NOT A STATEMENT ABOUT THE PULL REQUEST. Nothing has been verified;"
                " re-run with --verify-only once the remote settles.")
        waited = True
        time.sleep(MAIN_ADVANCE_POLL_S)
    if waited:
        print(f"gh-merge-verified: waited for origin main to advance past {main_before[:12]}")

    rc, base, _ = run(["git", "merge-base", head, main_sha])
    base = base.strip()
    if rc != 0 or not base:
        die(4, f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — no merge base between PR {pr} and main")

    rc, names, _ = run(["git", "diff", "--name-only", f"{base}..{head}"])
    files = [f for f in names.splitlines() if f.strip()]
    if not files:
        die(4, f"gh-merge-verified: COULD NOT DETERMINE — PR {pr} changes no files against its own",
            "  merge base, so there is nothing whose presence could be checked.")

    # THREE OUTCOMES PER FILE, NOT TWO. Comparing only "main == pr head" decays:
    # once main edits a file for an unrelated reason, a landing that DID happen
    # starts reporting as missing. Measured on PR 2288, which landed cleanly and
    # then had one of its files edited hours later by a different change -- the
    # two-state check called it unlanded.
    #
    # The base blob is what discriminates:
    #   main == head  -> ARRIVED. The change is present.
    #   main == base  -> ABSENT.  Main still holds the pre-change content, so
    #                             this file's change definitely did not land.
    #   neither       -> MOVED ON. Someone changed it after, or instead. Cannot
    #                             be decided from blobs; a human must read it.
    arrived: list[str] = []
    absent: list[str] = []
    moved: list[str] = []
    for f in files:
        _, a, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{head}:{f}"])
        rc_b, b, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{main_sha}:{f}"])
        rc_c, c, _ = run(["git", "rev-parse", f"{base}:{f}"])
        a, b, c = a.strip(), b.strip(), c.strip()
        if rc_b == 0 and a == b:
            arrived.append(f)
        elif rc_c == 0 and rc_b == 0 and b == c:
            absent.append(f)
        elif rc_b != 0 and rc_c != 0:
            absent.append(f)
        else:
            moved.append(f)

    missing = absent + moved
    landed = len(arrived)
    write_receipt(receipt_path, {
        **resolution,
        "pr": pr,
        "repo": repo,
        "main_sha": main_sha,
        "files": files,
        "arrived": arrived,
        "absent": absent,
        "moved_on": moved,
    })
    print(f"gh-merge-verified: PR {pr} ({repo})")
    print(f"  pr head    {head}"
          + (f"  (corroborated against {resolution['corroborated_against'][:12]})"
             if resolution["corroborated"] else "  (UNCORROBORATED)"))
    print(f"  origin/main {main_sha}")
    print(f"  files changed by the branch: {len(files)}")
    print(f"    arrived  (main == pr head): {len(arrived)}")
    print(f"    absent   (main == merge base, change definitely not landed): {len(absent)}")
    print(f"    moved on (main matches neither, undecidable from blobs): {len(moved)}")

    if missing:
        print("", file=sys.stderr)
        verdict = "NOT LANDED" if absent else "UNDECIDABLE"
        print(f"gh-merge-verified: REFUSED ({verdict}) — {len(missing)} of {len(files)} changed",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  file(s) are not present on origin/main:", file=sys.stderr)
        for f in absent[:20]:
            print(f"      ABSENT   {f}   (main still holds the pre-change content)", file=sys.stderr)
        for f in moved[:20]:
            print(f"      MOVED ON {f}   (changed after, or instead — read it)", file=sys.stderr)
        if len(missing) > 40:
            print(f"      ... and {len(missing) - 40} more", file=sys.stderr)
        print("", file=sys.stderr)
        print("", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  ⚠️ AND IF YOU PUSHED SECONDS AGO, SUSPECT A STALE PULL REF FIRST.", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  refs/pull/N/head can still read the OLD sha immediately after a push --", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  measured twice on 2026-08-25, and it broke two pull requests. This tool", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  reads that ref, so a stale read compares against the wrong head and can", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  report a real landing as ABSENT. Re-run in a few seconds; if the verdict", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  changes, the first answer was the race, not the truth.", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  EXIT 3 THEREFORE MEANS CHECK BY HAND, NOT THAT THE MERGE FAILED.", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  ⚠️ READ THIS, DO NOT AUTOMATE ON IT. Content-identity is sound in one", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  direction only: identical files prove the change arrived, but differing", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  files can also mean the change was amended during review, or that main", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  moved the same lines for another reason. What is certain is that you may", file=sys.stderr)
        print("  NOT record this as landed on the strength of the merge output alone.", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(3)

    print(f"  VERIFIED: every file the branch touches is byte-identical on origin/main.")
    # ⚠️ THE DENOMINATOR IS DERIVED FROM THE PULL REF, SO A STALE READ MAKES THIS
    # SENTENCE TRUE ABOUT A SUBSET NOBODY NAMED. refs/pull/N/head can read the old
    # sha for a window after a push; everything above is derived from it — the
    # merge base, and then the file set. A stale head yields FEWER files, they may
    # all be on main, and this exits 0. That is a false GREEN, and unlike the
    # false-ABSENT case it does not tell you to look.
    #
    # It cannot be corroborated here without breaking a deliberate property: the
    # verify-only path makes NO gh call, which is what lets it run on boxes whose
    # `gh` cannot reach the API. Asking the API to confirm the head would be such
    # a call. So the count is printed and the caveat is stated, rather than a
    # check being faked.
    print(f"  ⚠️ over {len(files)} file(s), derived from refs/pull/{pr}/head. If this PR was",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("  pushed seconds ago that ref can be STALE, making the file set too small and",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("  this success true only of a subset. Confirm the count against the PR's real",
          file=sys.stderr)
    print("  file list, or re-run once the ref has converged.", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(0)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
